<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Every user-visible string lives here from the first commit. No literal ever
  enters a composable — AM11 adds values-tr/ and a string sweep at that point
  would be the expensive way to do it.

  NAMING CONVENTION, and why it exists:

    nav_*        bottom-navigation destination labels
    screen_*     screen titles and placeholder body text
    settings_*   labels and descriptions inside Settings
    action_*     buttons and menu items
    error_*      messages surfaced per hard rule 6
    field_*      a watch attribute used as a FORM LABEL or table header
    enum_<group>_*  one suggested value within a specific enum* list

  The last two are the important pair. The desktop gets away with a flat
  vocabulary because Qt translation contexts disambiguate — the same English
  word can carry different Turkish translations in different contexts. Android
  string resources are one flat namespace with no such mechanism, and several
  terms genuinely appear in more than one place: "Power Reserve" is both a
  movement field label and a complication value; "GMT", "None", "Other",
  "Chronograph" and "Silicone" each appear in two or three separate enum lists.

  Collapsing those into one key would produce wrong labels in Turkish, and the
  damage would only become visible in AM11 when the sweep is already frozen.
  Hence: field_power_reserve and enum_complication_power_reserve are always
  distinct keys, even when the English happens to match.

  See android/docs/ANDROID-STRINGS.md.
-->
